@* The chrome every screen in the install flow shares: the bar across the top, the community
   footer across the bottom, the dialog the bar's Feedback button opens, and the centred column
   the page's own content sits in.

   A component the pages wrap themselves in, rather than markup in MainLayout, because /updates,
   /licenses and /debug are side trips rather than steps and keep their own plain page -- hosting
   the chrome in the layout would mean the layout inspecting the current route to decide.

   It exists at all because the moving parts were hand-assembled per page: a screen that added
   <TopBar> and forgot <FeedbackDialog> would get a Feedback button that silently did nothing, and
   no test would have caught it. *@

<div class="screen">
    <TopBar OnFeedback="() => _feedbackOpen = true" />

    <div class="screen-body">
        @ChildContent
    </div>

    @* The footer belongs to the shell, not to the welcome screen that used to own it, for the
       same reason the bar does: this is the window's furniture, and furniture that appears and
       disappears as you move through a flow reads as the app changing shape under you. *@
    <CommunityLinks />

    <FeedbackDialog Visible="_feedbackOpen" OnClose="() => _feedbackOpen = false" />
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    private bool _feedbackOpen;
}
